          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A posterior duodenal ulcer causes torrential upper gastrointestinal bleeding by </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A posterior duodenal ulcer causes torrential upper gastrointestinal bleeding by eroding which artery?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Left gastric artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The left gastric artery runs along the lesser curvature, arising from the coeliac trunk. It is not anatomically related to posterior duodenal ulcers.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Gastroduodenal artery</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The gastroduodenal artery passes directly behind the first part of the duodenum, making it vulnerable to erosion by a posterior duodenal ulcer — a classic cause of massive upper GI haemorrhage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Right gastro-epiploic artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Although the right gastro-epiploic is a branch of the gastroduodenal artery, it courses along the greater curvature and is not the vessel eroded by a posterior duodenal ulcer.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Splenic artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The splenic artery supplies the greater curvature via the left gastro-epiploic and short gastric arteries, but lies along the pancreas — not behind the duodenum.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the arterial supply: the gastroduodenal artery passes behind the first part of the duodenum, placing it at direct risk of erosion by posterior duodenal ulcers."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The gastroduodenal artery's position directly behind the first part of the duodenum is a key surgical anatomy relationship. Erosion by a posterior ulcer is one of the commonest causes of catastrophic upper GI bleeding."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
