          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>An infant with hypertrophic pyloric stenosis has persistent non-bilious vomiting</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>An infant with hypertrophic pyloric stenosis has persistent non-bilious vomiting. What is the expected metabolic disturbance?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In IHPS, chloride is lost through vomiting of hydrochloric acid, producing hypochloraemia and alkalosis — not acidosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Respiratory alkalosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Respiratory alkalosis results from hyperventilation. In IHPS, the alkalosis is metabolic, caused by loss of gastric acid through persistent vomiting.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Repeated vomiting of HCl-rich gastric contents depletes hydrogen and chloride ions. The kidneys then excrete potassium to conserve hydrogen, producing hypokalaemia and paradoxical aciduria.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lactic acidosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Lactic acidosis occurs in tissue hypoperfusion. In IHPS, the primary disturbance is a metabolic alkalosis from persistent gastric acid loss.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: the infant vomits hydrochloric acid. Loss of H⁺ and Cl⁻ produces a hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis. The kidneys compensate by retaining H⁺ and losing K⁺, adding hypokalaemia."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "This triad — hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis — is pathognomonic of IHPS. Correcting it with intravenous normal saline and potassium before surgery is essential to prevent perioperative apnoea and arrhythmia."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
