          "label": "<p>A patient presents with sudden severe epigastric pain and a rigid abdomen. Which</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient presents with sudden severe epigastric pain and a rigid abdomen. Which investigation would confirm a perforated peptic ulcer?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Endoscopy is contraindicated in suspected perforation due to the risk of further peritoneal contamination and worsening the perforation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Erect chest X-ray</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Free gas (pneumoperitoneum) appears as a black crescent under the diaphragm on an erect chest X-ray, confirming perforation. This is the first-line investigation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Barium swallow</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Barium is contraindicated in suspected perforation — leakage of barium into the peritoneal cavity causes severe chemical peritonitis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Abdominal ultrasound</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Abdominal ultrasound is the confirmatory test for infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis, not for perforated peptic ulcer. An erect chest X-ray is the correct first-line test here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "In suspected perforation, the first investigation is an erect chest X-ray looking for free gas (a black crescent) under the diaphragm. Endoscopy and barium studies are contraindicated."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "An erect chest X-ray demonstrating free gas under the diaphragm is a rapid, widely available test that confirms perforation. The clinical triad of sudden pain, rigidity, and pneumoperitoneum is classic."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
